                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[H1]LEGO Star Wars: The Skywalker Saga — More Galaxy, Less LEGO Magic[/h1]\n\n[b]Honestly? I'll take The Complete Saga over this any day of the year. [/b]\n\nI went into this game cautiously optimistic. Hearing that it had a revamped aiming and shooting system, reworked mechanics, and a whole new approach to LEGO Star Wars gameplay sounded promising! I was genuinely looking forward to seeing how the formula had evolved since LEGO Star Wars: The Complete Saga and LEGO Star Wars III: The Clone Wars.\n\nSadly, I ended up enjoying this game a lot less than I expected.\n\n[b]My biggest problem? THE VOICE ACTING. AGAIN. [/b]\n\nI know fully voiced LEGO games have been around for years now, but that doesn't mean I'm ever going to stop missing the old style.\n\nThe genius of classic LEGO humor was that the characters didn't need dialogue. They communicated through exaggerated expressions, ridiculous physical comedy, and wonderfully stupid LEGO-related shenanigans. You could understand exactly what a character was feeling without a single spoken word, and somehow that made everything ten times funnier.\n\nThe silence was part of the charm. The expressions were the punchline. The animations did the storytelling.\n\nAnd now? Characters just talk. And talk. And talk some more.\n\nIt makes the humor feel so much flatter to me. The personality that used to come through in those hilarious little nonverbal moments just doesn't hit the same way when everything is spelled out through dialogue.\n\nI know some people love the voice acting, and fair enough! But for me, it takes away a huge part of what made the older LEGO games so special. I don't need LEGO characters explaining the joke when watching them silently do something incredibly stupid was already the joke. \n\n[b]And then there's the open world... or should I say, the open-world marathon. [/b]\n\nI understand why the developers wanted to create huge, explorable planets. The worlds look fantastic, the character designs are more detailed, and everything feels much closer to the actual Star Wars universe than in the older games.\n\nBut bigger doesn't automatically mean better.\n\nThere is just SO MUCH TRAVERSAL. Running around enormous areas to reach the next objective gets tedious, and instead of feeling like an exciting adventure across the galaxy, it sometimes feels like I'm spending half my time commuting to the fun part.\n\nApparently, they learned the wrong lessons from Jedi: Fallen Order and Jedi: Survivor. \n\nI don't mind exploration when it feels rewarding, but here, the sheer amount of traveling often feels like padding rather than meaningful gameplay. I'd rather have tighter, more focused levels packed with creative set pieces, clever puzzles, and classic LEGO comedy than a massive map that makes me wish my character had a speeder with a skip-travel button.\n\n[b]Yes, the game looks better. But does it FEEL better? Not to me. [/b]\n\nI'd happily trade the prettier graphics, more elaborate worlds, and voiced dialogue for the old games' visual comedy, focused level design, and that unmistakable LEGO personality.\n\n[b]The Skywalker Saga tries to do more, but somehow feels like less. [/b]\n\nMaybe that's a bit harsh, and I can absolutely see why someone who prefers modern open-world games would enjoy it more than I did. But as someone who grew up loving the older LEGO Star Wars games, this just didn't scratch the same itch.\n\nGive me The Complete Saga. Give me LEGO Star Wars III: The Clone Wars. Give me silent characters communicating exclusively through increasingly ridiculous facial expressions and slapstick comedy.\n\nI miss that magic, and this game reminded me just how special it was.\n\n[b]5/10[/b] The galaxy is bigger, the graphics are prettier, and the characters can talk... but I still want my old LEGO Star Wars back.\n\nThe Force is strong with the originals. This one? Not so much.",
